<p>To get your event covered by local news in New Mexico, give a newsroom a real story, not just a date on a calendar. Find the human angle, send a short media advisory to the right people about a week ahead, follow up the day before and the morning of, and make the event easy to film when a crew shows up. Reporters and assignment editors are deciding fast, so the easier you make their yes, the better your odds.</p>

<p>You don't need a PR firm or a famous guest to pull this off. Small towns and local nonprofits land coverage all the time because their stories are specific, visual and close to home. Here's how to do it step by step, whether you're in Albuquerque, Santa Fe, Las Cruces or a village in the Mora Valley.</p>

<h2>What makes local TV news want to cover an event?</h2>

<p>Stations aren't looking for events. They're looking for stories their viewers will care about tonight. Your event is the place where that story happens. Before you write a word to a newsroom, answer these questions honestly:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Who is it about?</strong> A person with a reason to be there makes a story. A volunteer who's run the event for decades, a family keeping a tradition alive, a student who started something new.</li>
  <li><strong>Why now?</strong> A first, a last, an anniversary, a comeback, a season, a deadline. Timing gives an editor a reason to send someone today instead of someday.</li>
  <li><strong>Why here?</strong> Local history, local culture, a local problem being solved. New Mexico is full of stories nobody outside the community has heard yet.</li>
  <li><strong>What will it look like on camera?</strong> TV needs moving pictures. Hands at work, crowds, animals, dancing, music, a place most people never get to see.</li>
</ul>

<p>The <a href="https://toolkit.afterschoolalliance.org/sustaining-a-program/media-tools-for-afterschool/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Afterschool Alliance media toolkit</a> sums up what tends to get covered: events, issues that affect the audience, human interest, and local angles on bigger stories. If your pitch hits one of those squarely, you're in a much better spot.</p>

<h2>Media advisory vs. press release: which do you send?</h2>

<p>For an upcoming event, send a <strong>media advisory</strong>. It's a short notice that tells a newsroom exactly what's happening so they can decide whether to send a crew. A <strong>press release</strong> reads like a finished news story and fits news that's already happened or a formal announcement.</p>

<h3>What to put in a media advisory</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A plain headline</strong> that says what's happening and where. "Mora Valley mills open to the public for a two day tour" beats a clever slogan.</li>
  <li><strong>Who, what, when, where and why</strong> as short lines or bullets. Include the full address and where crews should park.</li>
  <li><strong>The visuals.</strong> List what a camera will see and the best time to catch it.</li>
  <li><strong>Who's available for interviews,</strong> with names and roles. If anyone speaks Spanish on camera, say so. That matters to stations with Spanish language audiences.</li>
  <li><strong>One media contact</strong> with a cell number that will actually be answered, including the morning of the event.</li>
  <li><strong>A link to photos</strong> or a short video so they can see the story before they commit.</li>
</ul>

<p>Keep it to one page. Paste it in the body of the email instead of only attaching it, and put the date and town in the subject line.</p>

<h2>Who to contact at a local station</h2>

<p>Every station's setup is a little different, so look at its website for a news tip or contact page and use the channel it asks for. Beyond that general inbox, a few people matter most:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The assignment desk</strong> decides what crews cover each day. This is where your advisory and your day-of follow up go.</li>
  <li><strong>Reporters who cover your kind of story.</strong> Watch the station for a week and notice who does community features, food, culture, education or your part of the state.</li>
  <li><strong>Morning show producers.</strong> Morning newscasts often have more room for community segments, live shots and guests.</li>
</ul>

<p>Don't stop at TV. Newspapers, public radio, local magazines, community calendars and local news sites all count, and a story in one often leads to a call from another. Keep a simple list of names and emails and update it, because reporters change beats and stations often.</p>

<h2>When to pitch: a simple timeline</h2>

<ol>
  <li><strong>About a week out:</strong> send the media advisory to the newsroom and the reporters you picked.</li>
  <li><strong>A day or two before:</strong> send a short follow up with any new details, like a guest confirmed or the forecast.</li>
  <li><strong>The morning of:</strong> call or email the assignment desk with a two line reminder and the best time for a crew to arrive.</li>
  <li><strong>That afternoon or the next day:</strong> send a recap with a few strong photos to anyone who couldn't make it.</li>
</ol>

<p>Newsrooms work on tight timelines, and breaking news can pull a crew away at the last minute. That's normal. A friendly reminder on the day is often what gets you on the schedule.</p>

<h2>Make your event easy to cover on the day</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Have something happening</strong> when the crew arrives. If they come at 9 and the action starts at noon, they'll leave with nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick one spokesperson</strong> who can explain the story in a few sentences and stay calm on camera. Have a second person ready if the first is busy.</li>
  <li><strong>Make your brand visible but natural.</strong> A banner behind the interview spot, consistent signs, shirts on volunteers. If the event name is easy to read, it's easy to mention.</li>
  <li><strong>Greet media at the entrance</strong> and walk them to the good shots. Know where the power outlets, shade and quiet spots are.</li>
  <li><strong>Get permission</strong> from people who'll be featured, especially families with kids.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A real example from the Mora Valley</h2>

<p>The Valley of the Mills Association had a two day tour and a story worth telling. Media pitches, event branding and a custom visitor app turned that tour into statewide coverage, including a KRQE feature. The event sold out, and the buzz outlasted it.</p>

<p>The coverage didn't come from luck. The pitch, the look of the event and the visitor experience all told the same story, so when reporters showed up, it was obvious what to film and why it mattered. You can see more of that work on our <a href="/social-media-marketing">social media and public relations services page</a>.</p>

<blockquote><p>"I'm learning social media marketing from the best! You have made a big difference and are a great mentor"</p><cite>Valley of the Mills Association, Mora, NM</cite></blockquote>

<h2>Turn one news story into weeks of marketing</h2>

<p>Coverage is a great moment, but most of its value comes after it airs.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Share the segment</strong> on your social channels and tag the station and reporter. Thank them publicly.</li>
  <li><strong>Add it to your website</strong> with a short "as seen on" note and a link to the story.</li>
  <li><strong>Send it to your email list,</strong> sponsors and partners. It's proof that your event matters.</li>
  <li><strong>Use it in next year's pitch.</strong> A station that covered you once is more likely to come back, especially if you bring a new angle.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you want your own photos and posts to keep that momentum going, our guide to <a href="/blog/grow-instagram-local-business">growing on Instagram without paid ads</a> covers the steady posting that turns one big moment into a following.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes that keep events off the news</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Pitching the event instead of the story.</strong> "Annual fall festival this Saturday" is a listing. "Three generations keep a village tradition going" is a story.</li>
  <li><strong>Sending a long attachment with no summary.</strong> Busy editors skim. Put the essentials in the email itself.</li>
  <li><strong>Giving a contact no one answers.</strong> If a producer calls at 7 a.m. and gets voicemail, they move on.</li>
  <li><strong>Pitching too early and never following up.</strong> An email from three weeks ago is easy to forget.</li>
  <li><strong>Overselling.</strong> Be honest about what's happening. Reporters remember who wasted their time and who didn't.</li>
</ul>

